<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Malindi</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1631 CE (approx.) · capital Malindi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Swahili city-state on the Kenyan coast, famous as the place that welcomed Vasco da Gama and became Portugal's first ally in East Africa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Malindi grew as a Muslim trading town on the Kenyan coast from about the 12th century, part of the network of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> ports between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-mogadishu">Mogadishu</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa</a>. In the mid-1410s envoys from Malindi brought a giraffe to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He's</a> fleet later carried them home.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Malindi was ruled by a sultan and merchant elite who traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, gold and slaves with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its long rivalry with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa</a> shaped its politics. In 1498 its ruler welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> and provided a pilot to guide him to India, and da Gama's stone pillar still stands. Thereafter it was a loyal ally and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1593 the Portuguese moved their base to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa</a> and installed the Malindi dynasty there, after which Malindi declined. The dynasty's rule ended after its sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-al-hasan">Yusuf ibn al-Hasan</a> revolted against the Portuguese at Mombasa in 1631. The town was largely abandoned until revived in the 19th century under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Zanzibar</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/malindi.jpg" alt="Malindi" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Mgiganteus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pillar_of_Vasco_da_Gama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1631 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Malindi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kenya">Kenya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Malindi, Melinde</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1366&amp;focus=malindi" aria-label="Malindi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5794 2298 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Malindi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6114 2554L4773 1044M6114 2554L6101 2585"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0M6101 2585h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6114 2554h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6114 2554h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1366&amp;focus=malindi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
